    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>An Ottoman officer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Salonica</a> who rose to prominence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a>, Mustafa Kemal led the Turkish National Movement against the partition of the country after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and won a victory confirmed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lausanne">Treaty of Lausanne</a> of 1923.</li><li>As first president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey</a> from 1923 to 1938 he abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, closed the religious courts and orders, introduced a secular civil code and the Latin-based alphabet, and gave women equal civil and political rights.</li><li>He ruled a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> that suppressed its opponents and pressed minorities such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> to assimilate; praised as a nation-builder and an inspiration to anti-colonial movements, he is also condemned for repression and for continuing the persecution of Christian minorities begun by the CUP.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When Mustafa Kemal asked linguists in 1928 how long a new Latin-based alphabet would take to introduce, most said three to five years; he is said to have answered, &quot;We shall do it in three to five months.&quot; The alphabet was introduced on 1 November 1928.</p></aside>
    </section>

